@startuml
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Segoe UI
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam ArrowColor #0b7a75
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #0b7a75
skinparam DatabaseBorderColor #6b7280
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #f3f4f6
skinparam NoteBorderColor #9ca3af

skinparam ActivityBorderColor #0b7a75
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor #9ca3af

title Resolving what somebody said into taxonomy codes: one funnel, two catalogue partitions, and the model held to what the catalogue returned

start

:the request, plus the last five turns;

if (which partition of the catalogue?) then (a care giver)
  :Section = Individual;
  :prompts: specialty_normalize_system_prompt\nspecialty_filter_system_prompt;
else (a facility)
  :Section = Non-Individual;
  :prompts: facility_normalize_system_prompt\nfacility_filter_system_prompt;
endif

note right
  The two resolutions differ in exactly two things: the
  partition the query searches and the prompt text the two
  model stages read. The stage sequence, the score floor,
  the intersection and the single catch point are shared,
  so the two cannot drift apart.
end note

:normalise — one model call, two answers:\nsearch_term (what to look for)\ncomplaint (what is wrong);
note right
  Emitting one string for both put a specialty name where
  the person's problem belongs: "orthodontist" is who you
  see, not what you have. An empty search_term is refused
  and re-asked rather than accepted.
end note

:embed the search_term\nwith the canonical model;

:$vectorSearch SpecialtyMetaData\nfilter { Section: <partition> };

:drop Deactivated and inactive rows\nkeep cosine >= 0.55;

if (any candidates?) then (none)
  :no matching specialty;
  stop
endif

:second-stage model narrows the candidates\ncode, score, display name, grouping, definition;
note right
  The candidate line carries grouping and definition
  because NUCC gives two codes the same display name in
  six cases, and a model shown the same words twice has
  nothing to choose on.
end note

:intersect the answer with the codes\nthe catalogue actually returned;
note right
  This is what makes a semantic step safe to have at all:
  the model chooses among registry facts and cannot invent
  one. A code it returns that was not a candidate is dropped.
end note

if (anything kept?) then (none)
  :no matching specialty;
  stop
endif

:re-rank by cosine, rank 1..n\nemit code, display name,\ncan_prescribe, homeopathic;

stop

floating note
  Every stage fails loudly and is caught once, in
  find_specialties, which answers with the real upstream
  cause rather than a substitute value. There is no
  degraded path: an unresolved complaint is not a search
  across every specialty.
end note

@enduml
